jane_support: FYI escalation — customer on Vercel Edge says our TS SDK blows up in the browser: preflight 403 CORS when SDK sets Authorization header. They report v0.8.2. Repro steps below.

jane_support: Repro steps from customer (copied):
```
// vite + next-edge on Vercel
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_PUBLIC_KEY })
await client.generate({ prompt: 'hi' })
```

Result: browser shows "Access to fetch at 'https://api.redwood.example/generate' from origin 'https://myapp.vercel.app' has been blocked by CORS policy: Request header field Authorization is not allowed by Access-Control-Allow-Headers in preflight response."

tom_dev: Thanks — do we know whether the SDK tries to set any forbidden headers or uses fetch polyfills that transform headers? Also which bundler (vite/webpack) exact config?

jane_support: They're on Vercel + vite; client is bundled into edge function. They said it worked when moving the call to a Node serverless function, but fails when running in the browser/edge runtime.

alex_devex: Quick checklist: 1) Edge runtimes (Vercel/CF Workers) block some headers on preflight. 2) Browsers send preflight for any custom headers like Authorization unless CORS response includes them. 3) Server must include Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization,Content-Type. Does our hosted API allow Authorization in CORS for browser origins?

lucy_eng: We currently default CORS to allow Authorization for origins we recognize, but some customers hit our CDN layer which strips the header on OPTIONS. Could be CDN or intermediate proxy returning preflight without the header.

jane_support: Customer says direct curl works: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" https://api.redwood.example/generate -> 200. Only browser fails.

tom_dev: That matches an OPTIONS preflight problem. Two angles: (A) server needs to respond to OPTIONS with Access-Control-Allow-Headers including Authorization; (B) workaround client-side: avoid sending Authorization from browser — use a short-lived browser token that the SDK reads from a cookie or use a backend exchange.

alex_devex: Also SDK v0.8.2 has a known issue: when bundlers treeshake the polyfilled Headers object we sometimes end up passing a Headers instance into CF Worker fetch which fails serialization during preflight. That was fixed in v0.9.0 by switching to a plain-record headers object in edge builds. Can you confirm the customer is on exact v0.8.2?

jane_support: Customer confirmed v0.8.2. They can try upgrading.

lucy_eng: Proposed immediate support steps for the customer: 1) Upgrade to SDK >= 0.9.0 (patch fixes Headers -> plain object for edge). 2) If upgrade is not possible, proxy calls through a small serverless function on the same origin (so browser doesn't need Authorization header). 3) Check Vercel/CND rules — ensure OPTIONS at CDN edge returns Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization.

tom_dev: For repro, I pushed a minimal Vite + edge example to reproduce locally. Use this snippet in a Next/edge route and set runtime:'edge':
```
// app/api/test/route.ts (Next.js edge)
export const runtime = 'edge'
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_PUBLIC_KEY })
export async function GET() {
  try {
    const r = await client.generate({ prompt: 'hello' })
    return new Response(JSON.stringify(r))
  } catch (e) {
    return new Response(e.message || 'err', { status: 500 })
  }
}
```
This reproduces the preflight error if the origin and CDN don't allow Authorization.

alex_devex: Also note: Cloudflare Workers / Vercel Edge deserialize headers differently — if SDK builds include the node Headers polyfill (node-fetch), CF may consider the header non-plain and drop it. The fix path is: (1) ensure build target for edge is ESM and no node polyfills, (2) send headers as plain object: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}` }. We updated the SDK bundler config in 0.9.0 to guarantee that for edge bundles.

jane_support: Customer tried upgrading to 0.9.1 and says CORS 403 is gone — thanks. They still see a different error intermittently: 429 coming from our rate limiter when calling from many clients in parallel. Any quick notes?

lucy_eng: 429 is expected if many clients hit with short-lived browser tokens — recommend client-side jitter/backoff and consolidated requests. We can also add a rate-limit bypass for verified enterprise origins if needed.

tom_dev: I'll open an internal issue to audit CDN preflight behavior and confirm our hosted CORS config explicitly includes Authorization and the correct Vary headers.

